<Layout title="Build journal" description="How CryptoOracle was developed with human direction and Codex: the architecture, real deployment checks, failed assumptions and next experiments.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-hero"><p class="eyebrow">THE DEVELOPMENT JOURNAL</p><h1>Built with AI.<br /> <em>Checked against reality.</em></h1><p>A record of the engineering decisions behind the experiment — including the results we did not hope for.</p></header>
    <div class="journal-intro"><p>CryptoOracle is a project by <a href="https://www.moinsen.dev/en" rel="external noreferrer">Ulrich Diedrichsen / Moinsen</a>. Ulrich defines the questions and product decisions. Codex assists with implementation, investigation, tests and deployment.</p><p>AI assistance is part of the development process. Claims on this site are based on recorded measurements and checks, with their limits stated explicitly. The <a href="https://github.com/moinsen-dev/crypto-oracle" rel="external noreferrer">public GitHub repository</a> contains the research service, this website, tests and setup instructions. Credentials, private operator notes, databases and model weights are excluded.</p></div>
    <ol class="journal">
      <li><div class="journal-date"><time datetime="2026-09-17">17 SEP 2026</time><span>01 / FOUNDATIONS</span></div><article><h2>Make a prediction we can revisit.</h2><p>The first build combined a three-asset watchlist, manually entered holdings, completed hourly candles and a pinned TimesFM model. Forecast inputs and outputs were recorded before their target times. FinBERT supplied headline tone alongside transparent rules for asset and event labels.</p><p>The architecture stayed small: Python, FastAPI, SQLite and a separate worker. Missing forecasts remain visible. No substitute model is presented under the TimesFM name.</p><div class="journal-result"><span>Engineering choice</span><strong>Preserve the evidence before improving the model.</strong></div></article></li>
      <li><div class="journal-date"><time datetime="2026-09-17">17 SEP 2026</time><span>02 / THE BASELINE</span></div><article><h2>The model did not win.</h2><p>The historical study grew from an initial 90-day run to 365 daily origins. Across BTC, ETH and SOL, the 24-hour TimesFM error was 5.2%, 6.1% and 9.3% higher than persistence.</p><p>Those findings are kept in the public record. The whole study is labelled exploratory, and no investment performance is inferred from a forecasting metric.</p><a class="text-link" href="/#evidence">Inspect the result <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article></li>
      <li><div class="journal-date"><time datetime="2026-09-17">17 SEP 2026</time><span>03 / REAL OPERATION</span></div><article><h2>Move from a laptop to a research server.</h2><p>The system was installed on a Hetzner AX41 in separate Docker web and worker services. Checks covered real model inference, authenticated API access, browser use, data retention across restarts and a consistent database backup.</p><p>Recorded browser checks included adding, reloading and removing a test holding, switching horizons and checking a narrow screen. These prove those interactions at the time of testing; they do not establish long-term availability or forecasting quality.</p><div class="journal-result"><span>Boundary</span><strong>The research dashboard remains private.</strong></div></article></li>
      <li><div class="journal-date"><time datetime="2026-09-18">18 SEP 2026</time><span>04 / JEV</span></div><article><h2>Add news evaluation without rewriting history.</h2><p>JEV was integrated through the AI SDK evaluation interface, using a small Node adapter alongside Python. Each headline receives structured answers about asset relevance, material relevance, tone and event type.</p><p>The first server check recorded eight successful annotations. Provider rate limits also occurred, leading to a persistent retry delay and bounded request attempts. The price experiment continues independently of this optional annotation stream.</p><p>The recorded validation at that stage included 23 Python tests, three SDK contract tests and browser checks. That demonstrates integration behavior, not calibrated classification quality.</p></article></li>
      <li><div class="journal-date"><time datetime="2026-09-18">18 SEP 2026</time><span>05 / INPUT TRUST</span></div><article><h2>Separate confidence from data quality.</h2><p>A public discussion raised the right challenge: a confident answer can still rely on a distorted price or delayed feed. The resulting design calls for independent venue checks, spread and depth limits and visible reasons for rejecting an observation.</p><p>The design specifies a virtual $10,000 portfolio with its own ledger, fixed risk rules, realistic costs and comparisons. It was implemented later the same day; the original forecasting experiment remains unchanged.</p><a class="text-link" href="/paper-portfolio/">Inspect the paper experiment <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article></li>
      <li><div class="journal-date"><time datetime="2026-09-18">18 SEP 2026</time><span>06 / THIS NOTEBOOK</span></div><article><h2>Make the method publicly inspectable.</h2><p>A dedicated Astro site presents the research in English. Cloudflare serves static files; the public build contains selected aggregate results, not the research database. Navigation, results and explanatory content work without contacting a model service.</p><p>The site has no advertising, visitor analytics, third-party fonts or contact form. Its privacy notice explains the remaining hosting-related processing and provides the operator’s contact details.</p><a class="text-link" href="/privacy/">Read how this site handles data <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article></li>
      <li><div class="journal-date"><time datetime="2026-09-18">18 SEP 2026</time><span>07 / PAPER TRADING</span></div><article><h2>Turn forecasts into accountable decisions.</h2><p>Three virtual portfolios now run on the research server: TimesFM with a JEV buy veto, TimesFM alone, and a buy-and-hold reference. Coinbase USD books are checked against Kraken and converted Binance quotes. Every decision preserves its inputs and every simulated order needs a later executable quote.</p><p>The first run produced three reference purchases. Both AI policies initially stayed in cash because their signals did not clear the fixed cost and uncertainty thresholds. These are recorded outcomes, not a demonstration of trading advantage.</p><p>An append-only journal records deposits, decisions, orders, fills, cancellations and valuations. A bounded authenticated export makes balances and recent trails public without exposing manual holdings or model credentials. Fault tests cover stale and distorted prices, thin books, point-in-time news, restart idempotency and cash accounting.</p><a class="text-link" href="/paper-portfolio/">Follow the live virtual portfolios ↗</a></article></li>
      <li><div class="journal-date"><time datetime="2026-09-18">18 SEP 2026</time><span>08 / A COMMON START</span></div><article><h2>Separate initial exposure from later decisions.</h2><p>The first experiment exposed a design choice: the reference was invested while the AI portfolios waited in cash for forecasts to clear their entry thresholds. That comparison measured different initial exposure as well as subsequent decisions.</p><p>A second version gives all three portfolios the same initial target: 20% BTC, 20% ETH, 20% SOL and 40% cash. The initial fills share quantities, later quotes and costs, and commit together. They are explicitly labelled as fixed setup trades. The AI strategies can subsequently reduce, exit or add under the existing rules; the reference holds.</p><p>The original experiment continues unchanged. Both versions retain independent journals, start dates and public views. This is a new prospective experiment, not a retrospective improvement to earlier returns. Equal initial holdings make the next question clearer; they do not establish that the AI adds value.</p><a class="text-link" href="/paper-portfolio/">Compare the experiments ↗</a></article></li>
      <li><div class="journal-date"><time datetime="2026-09-19">19 SEP 2026</time><span>09 / LEARN FROM THE RECORD</span></div><article><h2>A correct direction can still be a poor forecast.</h2><p>The new public forecast journal preserves the original reference, target price and deadline, then displays the actual outcome and error. A forecast can get the direction right and still miss the size of the move or its interval. Pending targets remain pending.</p><p>Data-quality checks now also protect learning labels: source revisions, missing same-hour references or disagreement across Binance, Coinbase and Kraken exclude an outcome from the new calibration learner. The earliest legacy inputs remain visible without being retrospectively certified.</p><p>A small, versioned calibration candidate learns from earlier verified errors after sufficient history exists. It issues separate future predictions and receives a fixed-window review against three references. Original TimesFM outputs and paper-trading rules stay intact. Recording this machinery is not evidence that it has already improved predictions.</p><a class="text-link" href="/forecasts/">Inspect the forecast and learning record ↗</a></article></li>
      <li><div class="journal-date"><time datetime="2026-09-19">19 SEP 2026</time><span>10 / PUBLIC SOURCE</span></div><article><h2>Give the experiment a home — and publish the code.</h2><p>CryptoOracle now lives at <a href="https://cryptooracle.moinsen.dev/">cryptooracle.moinsen.dev</a>. The same Cloudflare service and research ledger continue underneath, preserving the existing forecasts and virtual portfolio history.</p><p>The <a href="https://github.com/moinsen-dev/crypto-oracle" rel="external noreferrer">source repository at moinsen-dev</a> makes the implementation and tests inspectable. The website links directly to the code, with no embedded GitHub widgets or visitor tracking.</p></article></li>
    </ol>
    <div class="closing-note"><p class="eyebrow">THE NEXT CHAPTER</p><h2>More observations. Better questions.</h2><p>The next evidence comes from prospective observations: costs, blocked decisions, exposure, drawdowns and differences between the fixed policies. Earlier results remain in the record.</p></div>
  </div>
